{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said: Shall I hide…</b> The reason for His saying the expression <b>ha'mechasseh</b> (shall I hide) is that Abraham saw the third angel — that he had come about a matter for the world, and the matter was not known to him, as he knew from the actions of the two [other] angels: one had come to heal and one to announce to Sarah. And further, when they departed from him, he saw that two of them set their faces toward the road to Sodom, while Abraham was walking with them to send them off — behold, he sees that they have something to do, and the matter is hidden from him. Therefore HaShem said <b>Shall I hide from Abraham what I am doing</b> — meaning: before doing it. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanchuma) that it weighed on Abraham's heart concerning the waters of the Flood — why did HaShem not suspend it for them on account of the merit of the righteous who were among them? And according to their words, the verse intends to say <b>what I am doing</b> — meaning: what I am accustomed to do, for I do not destroy a nation unless there are no righteous in it." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend His saying <b>shall I hide</b> etc., to be that since Abraham saved the people of Sodom with his sword and his bow together with Lot, as stated in the previous portion, the matter concerns him — to know what will be done to them, and the matter should not be hidden from him in the language of concealment. For he supposes that the people whom he saved are still secure, while HaShem is destroying them without his knowledge. Therefore He said ״ha'mechasseh״ (shall I hide) etc. And there was no need to explain this reason, for it is a well-known matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:04.966018433Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}